message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Testosterone Raises PSA Across the Physiological Range, with Implications for Detection Bias in Prostate Cancer"
abstract: >-
  A two sample Mendelian randomization and Bayesian evidence synthesis study.
  Prostate specific antigen is transcribed under androgen receptor control and
  is used here as an in vivo bioassay of prostatic androgen signalling:
  genetically higher bioavailable testosterone raises PSA across the sampled
  physiological range, which bounds the androgen saturation model rather than
  refuting it. Between a third and a half of the genetic association with
  diagnosed prostate cancer is carried by the PSA channel, the residual direct
  effect is not distinguishable from the null, and in randomized trials
  testosterone therapy doubled prostate biopsies without increasing prostate
  cancer. The evidence does not establish absence of effect.
